wer back-half window and higher threshold require -- my front-loading (3.1% then 2.6%) is what reconciles 0.70 with 0.43, and it is the thing a reader should check me on. (iv) CQ2 = 47 is a JOINT including the China leg at 0.63, so it carries no direct implication for C beyond (iii). (v) C + D = 34 and A + B = 66: unlike Wave 1 I am NOT two-sided on inflation risk -- my C is nearly 3x my D, because the whole content of my lens is that the LEVEL risk is asymmetric upward even though the SKEW is as violent as Wave 1 says. (vi) Sums to exactly 100.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:52.587Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"US core PCE 12-month inflation prints at or below 2.0% for six consecutive months at any point in 2028-2030, with GDP-weighted OECD-38 core ex-EM also below 2.2% over the same stretch.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Kills the drifting-anchor plank that carries my whole payload. CQ2 falls from 47 to ~30 (OECD leg 0.70 -> 0.45); CQ6-C falls ~8 pp, redistributed ~5 to B and ~3 to D; CQ4 P50 down ~30 bp with a fatter left tail; hypothesis (c) rises from 38 toward 60, i.e. Wave 1 was right and I was wrong.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"US CPI core commodities (core goods) 12-month rate at or below 0.0% for calendar-year 2029 while Section 301/232-style tariff coverage remains in force at or above 2026 breadth.\",\"by_when\":\"Jan 31 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my single largest weight-level departure from Wave 1 (core goods 1.2% vs their 0.3-0.5%, worth +0.20 pp on the aggregate and the proof-of-concept for the nominal-rigidity channel). CQ2 -10 pp to ~37; CQ6-C -6 pp into B; CQ5 conjunct A down ~0.05.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"No G7 state legislates a statutory care-worker wage floor, sectoral award or funded pay settlement delivering a real uplift of 15% or more ph